        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Sámi</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">Sápmi, formerly called Lapland: northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and the Kola Peninsula of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>; centres include Guovdageaidnu (Kautokeino), Kárášjohka (Karasjok), Inari, Jokkmokk and Lovozero; once also southern Finland</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Arrived in Sápmi in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> or early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> (2nd–1st millennia BCE); the Sámi proto-language formed c. 2,000–2,500 years ago (Ante Aikio); reindeer nomadism from c. 1500 CE; ruled by the surrounding states from the 15th century; a living people</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the Sámi languages of the Uralic family; reindeer herding in siidas; Sea Sámi fishing; joik; duodji crafts and gákti clothing; the noaidi and painted drums; the board game tablut; the Sámi Parliaments, flag and National Day</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Roman and Greek mentions of the Fenni and Phinnoi; the account of Ohthere to King Alfred; Norse sagas; Johannes Schefferus’s Lapponia (1673); archaeology and place names; linguistics; genetic studies; Sámi joik, oral tradition and writing</span></div>
        </div>
