    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Sámi</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fur-trade.jpg" alt="Fur trade" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Fur trade <span class="kid-tale-cr">unknown / --Kuerschner 16:04, 24 March 2008 (UTC), Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>The Sámi are the Indigenous people of Sápmi, the far north of Norway, Sweden, Finland and Russia.</p><p>For more than 2,000 years they have fished, hunted, trapped and traded furs there.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cattle.jpg" alt="Cattle" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Cattle <span class="kid-tale-cr">dr. Plamen Ivanov, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>About 500 years ago, many settled by the coast and kept cattle, sheep and goats.</p><p>Some began herding reindeer, and today about one Sámi in ten is connected to herding.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/silver.jpg" alt="Silver" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Silver <span class="kid-tale-cr">Alchemist-hp, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</span></figcaption></figure><p>Nearby kingdoms taxed them, and Sweden forced some Sámi to work at a silver mine.</p><p>Missionaries burned their sacred drums, and later the Sámi language was banned in Norway’s schools.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cotton.jpg" alt="Cotton" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Cotton <span class="kid-tale-cr">കാക്കര, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Today the Sámi have their own parliaments, a flag and a national day.</p><p>Their gákti clothes, often of wool or cotton, show by their colours where the wearer comes from.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Sámi are the Indigenous people of Sápmi, speaking about ten related Uralic languages, and have lived in the far north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> or early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>.</li><li>For most of their history they lived by fishing, hunting, trapping and trade; large-scale reindeer herding grew from about 1500, and today about one Sámi in ten is connected to it.</li><li>Taxation, forced conversion, closed borders and assimilation policies bore down on them for centuries; since the 1970s Sámi parliaments, a flag, an anthem and a national day have marked a revival.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>All modern versions of the board game hnefatafl, usually sold as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> game, are based on the Sámi game tablut, the only tafl game whose rules survived relatively intact.</p></aside>
    </section>

